/* book Worked example: Q: A pre-tensioned beam 300 mm × 800 mm spans 12 m and carries a live load of 12 kN/m besides its self-weight (25 kN/m³). Pi = 1500 kN and η = 0.8. Permissible stresses: at transfer 16 N/mm² compression and 1.4 N/mm² tension; at service 15 N/mm² compression and no tension (Type 1). Check the midspan section with e = 250 mm, then with e = 220 mm, and find the range of eccentricity that works.
   1. A = 240,000 mm²; Z = 300 × 800²/6 = 32 × 10⁶ mm³. Self-weight = 0.3 × 0.8 × 25 = 6 kN/m; Mg = 6 × 12²/8 = 108 kN·m; Mq = 12 × 12²/8 = 216 kN·m; Pe = 0.8 × 1500 = 1,200 kN.
   2. e = 250 mm, transfer: Pi/A = 6.25, Pie/Z = 11.72, Mg/Z = 3.375. Top = −2.09 N/mm²: 2.09 N/mm² tension exceeds 1.4, so this fails. Bottom = 14.59 N/mm² ≤ 16, safe.
   3. e = 220 mm, transfer: Pie/Z = 10.312. Top = 6.25 − 10.312 + 3.375 = −0.688 N/mm² (tension 0.69 ≤ 1.4, safe); bottom = 13.188 N/mm² ≤ 16, safe.
   4. e = 220 mm, service: Pe/A = 5, Pee/Z = 8.25, Mt/Z = 10.125. Top = 6.875 N/mm² ≤ 15, safe; bottom = 3.125 N/mm², compression, so no tension: Type 1 met.
   5. Largest e from the transfer-top condition: e ≤ (Pi/A + Mg/Z + 1.4)Z/Pi = (6.25 + 3.375 + 1.4) × 32 × 10⁶/1,500,000 = 235.2 mm.
   6. Smallest e from the service-bottom condition: Pe/A + Pee/Z ≥ Mt/Z gives e ≥ (10.125 − 5) × 32 × 10⁶/1,200,000 = 136.7 mm.
   7. Section check: Zt ≥ (216 + 0.2 × 108) × 10⁶/(15 + 0.8 × 1.4) = 14.74 × 10⁶ and Zb ≥ 237.6 × 10⁶/(0.8 × 16) = 18.56 × 10⁶ mm³; 32 × 10⁶ is ample.
   Answer: With e = 250 mm the top fibre has 2.09 N/mm² tension at transfer, more than the 1.4 allowed. With e = 220 mm all four checks pass (transfer −0.69 and 13.19, service 6.88 and 3.12 N/mm²). Any e from 137 to 235 mm works. */
