/* book Worked example: Q: A post-tensioned beam has an end block 400 mm wide and 500 mm deep. A single tendon with jacking force 1000 kN is anchored at the centre through a 200 mm × 200 mm plate. fci = 40 N/mm² and Fe 415 links are used. Check the bearing stress and design the bursting reinforcement.
   1. Bearing: Apun = 200 × 200 = 40,000 mm². The largest concentric square on a 400 mm wide face is 400 × 400, so Abr = 160,000 mm²; √(Abr/Apun) = 2.
   2. Permitted fb = 0.48 × 40 × 2 = 38.4, but not more than 0.8 × 40 = 32 N/mm², so fb = 32 N/mm². Actual = 1,000,000/40,000 = 25 N/mm² ≤ 32: safe.
   3. Across the width (horizontal tension): 2ypo/2yo = 200/400 = 0.5; Fbst/Pk = 0.32 − 0.3 × 0.5 = 0.17; Fbst = 170 kN.
   4. Over the depth (vertical tension): 200/500 = 0.4; Fbst/Pk = 0.32 − 0.3 × 0.4 = 0.2; Fbst = 200 kN.
   5. Steel at 0.87 × 415 = 361.05 N/mm²: horizontal legs 170,000/361.05 = 471 mm²; vertical legs 200,000/361.05 = 554 mm².
   6. A closed 10 mm link gives two legs in each direction, 157 mm². Vertical demand 554/157 = 3.53 → 4 links (628 mm²), which also covers the 471 mm² horizontal demand.
   7. Place the 4 links at 140 mm centres, at 50, 190, 330 and 470 mm behind the face, so they span the 500 mm bursting zone, plus a 6 mm mesh at 75 mm centres just behind the face for spalling.
   Answer: Bearing 25 N/mm² against 32 N/mm² permitted. Bursting forces 200 kN (vertical) and 170 kN (horizontal) need 554 and 471 mm²; four 10 mm closed links (628 mm² each way) spread through the first 500 mm satisfy both. */
